  <title>Import and export</title>
  <p>An archive, a <file>.vbp</file> file, holds a project, to copy it to
  another computer, send it to a student, or keep it.</p>
  <p if:test="!target:html">The format of the archives is in
  <link href="man:virtualbricks-vbp(5)">virtualbricks-vbp(5)</link>.</p>
  <p if:test="target:html">The format of the archives is in
  <link href="https://www.virtualbricks.it/documentation/vbp-format.html">virtualbricks-vbp(5)</link>.</p>
  <section id="export">
    <title>Exporting a project</title>
    <p>Choose <guiseq><gui>Projects</gui><gui>Export…</gui></guiseq>, or
    <gui>Export…</gui> in the Projects window. The window asks where to save
    the archive and what to put in it, each with its size:</p>
    <list>
      <item><p>the project, its file and its README, always;</p></item>
      <item><p>the private copies of the disks of its machines, on at
      first;</p></item>
      <item><p>the images, off at first, since they are often large and the
      other computer may have them;</p></item>
      <item><p>the other files of the folder of the project.</p></item>
    </list>
    <figure>
      <title>The Export Project window</title>
      <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/export.png">
        <p>The Export Project window: Save as ~/new_project.vbp, and the
        project, its file and its README, included, with its size.</p>
      </media>
    </figure>
    <p>The export runs in the background, with its progress. You can stop it,
    or close the window and let it finish: the <gui>Logs</gui> window says
    when it's done. With <cmd>qemu-img</cmd> installed, the disks are
    compressed, and stay disks that QEMU can run.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="import">
    <title>Importing a project</title>
    <p>Choose <guiseq><gui>Projects</gui><gui>Import…</gui></guiseq>, or
    <gui>Import…</gui> in the Projects window, then the archive; or drop
    the archive on the main window or on the Projects window, from the file
    manager. One page shows what it holds:</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Name</gui></title>
        <p>The name of the new project: the one in the archive, or the next
        free one if it is taken. An import never replaces a project.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Images</gui></title>
        <p>For each image of the project: <gui>Copy</gui> it from the archive
        to the shared images, <gui>Use a file…</gui> of this computer, or
        <gui>Leave unset</gui>: the image stays without its file, until
        <link xref="images#menu"><gui>Find the File…</gui></link>, in the
        <gui>Images</gui> tab, gives it one. Each has a choice made already, so
        <gui>Import</gui> works at once. Above them, a bar while the window
        looks through the archive, then a line that says which images it has.
        A choice you make meanwhile stays.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>This computer's paths</gui></title>
        <p>The folders of QEMU and VDE of the project are replaced with yours
        when they don't exist here.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Open the project after the import</gui></title>
        <p>On at first.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>If you stop an import, or it fails, it removes what it wrote.
    <key>Esc</key> does as <gui>Cancel</gui>, which is <gui>Stop</gui> during
    the import. At the
    end, the <gui>Logs</gui> window says what needs your attention, as an
    image left unset. The archives of Virtualbricks 2.1 import too.</p>
  </section>
